(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["skin"]="<h1>Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-skin-blood-flow-is-mainly-about-heat-not-nutrition\">1. Skin blood flow is mainly about heat, not nutrition</h2>\n<p>Most tissues get the blood flow they need <strong>to feed their cells</strong>. The skin is different: its flow is controlled mainly to <strong>regulate body temperature</strong>. It can carry far more blood than its cells need.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hot:</strong> skin vessels <strong>dilate</strong>, so warm blood flows near the surface and heat is lost (flushed skin).</li>\n<li><strong>Cold:</strong> sympathetic nerves <strong>constrict</strong> skin vessels, so blood stays deep and heat is kept (pale, cool skin).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"ava\">Arteriovenous anastomoses (AVAs)</span></strong> are direct shortcuts from small arteries to veins, found in the fingers, toes, palms, ears and nose. When they open, a large volume of warm blood passes just under the skin, ideal for losing heat. They are controlled almost entirely by <strong>sympathetic</strong> nerves.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0802_Skin_DermalCirculation.png?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0802_Skin_DermalCirculation.png?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0802_Skin_DermalCirculation.png?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0802_Skin_DermalCirculation.png?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Detailed medical cutaway illustration of dermal circulation showing vessels in the skin and their relationship to superficial layers\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Blood vessels within the skin.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0802_Skin_DermalCirculation.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-skin-blood-flow-in-shock\">2. Skin blood flow in shock</h2>\n<p>In haemorrhage, sympathetic nerves <strong>shut down skin blood flow</strong>, diverting blood to the brain and heart. That is why a bleeding patient has <strong>cold, pale, clammy skin</strong>, often <strong>before</strong> blood pressure falls much. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Septic shock</strong> can be different early on: toxins dilate the vessels, so the skin may be <strong>warm and flushed</strong>. <strong>Skin temperature alone can't diagnose shock</strong>; it must be read with the rest of the patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–2</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> What mainly controls skin blood flow?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The oxygen needs of the skin cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The need to regulate body temperature</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The amount of sweat being produced</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The pressure in the skin's veins</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Skin flow can far exceed its nutritional needs. It is set mainly to keep or lose heat.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does a patient who is bleeding heavily have cold, pale skin?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Sympathetic nerves shut down skin flow to protect vital organs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Blood loss lowers body temperature everywhere equally</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The skin's arteriovenous anastomoses open widely</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Histamine constricts the skin vessels</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Sympathetic vasoconstriction diverts blood from the skin to the brain and heart.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Where are arteriovenous anastomoses mostly found, and what are they for?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the trunk, to supply nutrients to the skin</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the fingers, toes, ears and nose, to lose or keep heat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In deep muscles, to supply oxygen in exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the scalp, to drain blood from the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> AVAs in the extremities shunt warm blood under the skin when heat must be lost.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-the-triple-response-of-lewis\">3. The triple response of Lewis</h2>\n<p>Draw a blunt point <strong>firmly</strong> across the skin of the forearm. Within a minute, three things appear, in this order:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Sign</th>\n  <th>What you see</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>1. Red line</strong></td>\n  <td>A red line exactly along the stroke, within seconds</td>\n  <td><strong>Local capillaries dilate</strong> where the skin was injured</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>2. <span data-term=\"flare\">Flare</span></strong></td>\n  <td>A wider, blotchy red area spreading out around the line</td>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"axon-reflex\">Axon reflex</span></strong>: sensory nerve endings send signals along their own branches, dilating nearby arterioles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>3. <span data-term=\"wheal\">Wheal</span></strong></td>\n  <td>A raised, pale swelling along the line</td>\n  <td><strong>Histamine</strong> makes the capillaries <strong>leaky</strong>, so fluid leaks into the tissue (local oedema)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Lewis triple response — what each visible sign means</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 210\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three skin response illustrations: red line at stroke, broad surrounding red flare, and raised wheal swelling\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#293d5b\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"197\" height=\"145\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e8\" stroke=\"#caa18b\"/><rect x=\"221\" y=\"12\" width=\"197\" height=\"145\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e8\" stroke=\"#caa18b\"/><rect x=\"432\" y=\"12\" width=\"197\" height=\"145\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e8\" stroke=\"#caa18b\"/><path d=\"M36 90h145\" stroke=\"#d83e54\" stroke-width=\"7\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><ellipse cx=\"319\" cy=\"90\" rx=\"74\" ry=\"42\" fill=\"#ee8b8b\" opacity=\".6\"/><path d=\"M267 90h104\" stroke=\"#d83e54\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"530\" cy=\"94\" rx=\"67\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#e69e8d\" stroke=\"#cd7662\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M470 95Q530 51 590 95\" stroke=\"#fff6ed\" stroke-width=\"11\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"108\" y=\"180\">Red line</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"180\">Flare</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"180\">Wheal</text><text x=\"108\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"12\">Local dilation</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"12\">Axon reflex</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"12\">Fluid swelling</text></g></svg><p>A schematic of three related features after firm stroking: narrow local redness, wider surrounding redness and raised oedema. The brief white response to light stroking is separate from Lewis's triple response.</p></figure>\n\n<h3>Why does the flare spread? The axon reflex, step by step</h3>\n<p>A single sensory nerve fibre in the skin doesn't end at one point. Near the skin it <strong>splits into many branches</strong>, like the fingers of a hand, and together they cover a patch of skin a few centimetres across.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The stroke stimulates one branch.</strong> The firm stroke damages a few cells and stimulates the sensory nerve endings in the line of the stroke.</li>\n<li><strong>The signal travels up that branch.</strong> It travels towards the spinal cord, as any sensory signal would.</li>\n<li><strong>At the branch point, it also travels back down the other branches.</strong> An impulse travels in both directions along a nerve fibre. So when it reaches the fork, it carries on up towards the spinal cord <strong>and also runs back down every other branch</strong>, into skin that was never touched.</li>\n<li><strong>The endings of those branches release vasodilators.</strong> They release <strong><span data-term=\"substance-p\">substance P</span></strong> and <strong><span data-term=\"cgrp\">CGRP</span></strong> (calcitonin gene-related peptide). These dilate the nearby arterioles directly, and substance P also makes nearby <strong><span data-term=\"mast-cell\">mast cells</span> release histamine</strong>, which dilates them further.</li>\n<li><strong>The arterioles across the whole patch dilate, giving the flare.</strong> The redness spreads as far as that fibre's branches reach, which is why the flare is <strong>wider than the stroke but stops a few centimetres out</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why &quot;axon&quot; reflex?</strong> The whole loop happens <strong>within the branches of one nerve fibre</strong>. There is no synapse, and the spinal cord and brain are not needed. That makes it different from a true reflex arc, which passes through the spinal cord.</p>\n<p><strong>How do we know?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>local anaesthetic</strong> that blocks the nerve abolishes the flare. The red line and the wheal still appear, because they come from local injury and histamine, not from the nerve.</li>\n<li>After the sensory nerve has been <strong>cut and has degenerated</strong>, the flare disappears.</li>\n<li>Cutting the nerve <strong>above</strong> the branch point, closer to the spinal cord, does <strong>not</strong> stop the flare, because the loop doesn't need the spinal cord.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure axon-reflex-figure\" id=\"cvs26-axon\"><figcaption>Figure. The axon reflex: why the flare spreads</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 420\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"One sensory nerve fibre runs up towards the spinal cord. Near the skin it splits into four branches. Step 1: a firm stroke stimulates the left branch. Step 2: the impulse travels up that branch to the branch point. Step 3: it also travels back down the other three branches into skin that was not touched. Step 4: their endings release substance P and CGRP, which dilate the nearby arterioles, so a red flare spreads across the whole patch. The spinal cord is not needed for this loop.\"><defs><marker id=\"ax-head\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"ax-arrowhead\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"150\" y=\"12\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" class=\"ax-cord\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"37\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"ax-lab-sm\">Spinal cord</text><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"110\" class=\"ax-fibre ax-faint\"/><text x=\"212\" y=\"80\" class=\"ax-lab\">No spinal cord</text><text x=\"212\" y=\"97\" class=\"ax-lab\">needed</text><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"190\" class=\"ax-fibre\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"155\" text-anchor=\"end\" class=\"ax-lab-xs\">one sensory fibre</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"300\" width=\"360\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\" class=\"ax-skin\"/><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"335\" rx=\"172\" ry=\"28\" class=\"ax-flare\"/><g class=\"ax-fibre\"><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"300\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"300\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"300\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"300\"/></g><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"190\" r=\"5\" class=\"ax-node\"/><g class=\"ax-node\"><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"300\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"300\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"300\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"330\" cy=\"300\" r=\"4\"/></g><g class=\"ax-art\"><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"7\"/><ellipse cx=\"150\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"7\"/><ellipse cx=\"250\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"7\"/><ellipse cx=\"330\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"7\"/></g><path d=\"M44 314l8-8 8 8 8-8 8 8 8-8 8 8\" class=\"ax-stroke\"/><g class=\"ax-arrow\"><path d=\"M103 259L142 226\" marker-end=\"url(#ax-head)\"/><path d=\"M190 232L173 271\" marker-end=\"url(#ax-head)\"/><path d=\"M225 224L243 263\" marker-end=\"url(#ax-head)\"/><path d=\"M251 221L297 260\" marker-end=\"url(#ax-head)\"/></g><g class=\"ax-step\"><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"364\" r=\"10\"/><circle cx=\"96\" cy=\"234\" r=\"10\"/><circle cx=\"314\" cy=\"232\" r=\"10\"/></g><g text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"ax-step-num\"><text x=\"70\" y=\"368\">1</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"238\">2</text><text x=\"314\" y=\"236\">3</text></g><text x=\"86\" y=\"368\" class=\"ax-lab-sm\">Stroke</text><text x=\"372\" y=\"372\" text-anchor=\"end\" class=\"ax-lab-xs\">skin</text><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"403\" r=\"10\" class=\"ax-step\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"407\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"ax-step-num\">4</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"408\" class=\"ax-lab-sm\">Substance P + CGRP dilate arterioles → flare</text></svg><p>① The stroke stimulates one branch. ② The impulse travels up to the branch point. ③ It also runs back down the other branches. ④ Their endings release substance P and CGRP, so arterioles across the whole patch dilate. Original DOMiNANCE diagram.</p></figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Substance P and CGRP as the axon-reflex mediators, and the local-anaesthetic and nerve-degeneration findings, are standard physiology (Lewis, 1927; Guyton and Hall). The lecture names the axon reflex as the cause of the flare.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>White line, not part of the triple response.</strong> A <strong>light</strong> stroke produces only a brief <strong>pale line</strong>: the local vessels constrict for a moment.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory hook:</strong> <strong>red line</strong> = local redness; <strong>flare</strong> = spreading redness; <strong>wheal</strong> = raised swelling.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Section 3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does the flare spread beyond the line of the stroke?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Histamine diffuses widely through the skin</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. An axon reflex dilates arterioles through nearby nerve branches</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The brainstem sends dilating signals to the area</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The wheal pushes blood outwards into the surrounding skin</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Sensory nerve branches carry the signal sideways and release dilators, without involving the spinal cord or brain.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What causes the raised wheal?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Local capillaries dilating with blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Histamine making capillaries leaky, so fluid leaks into the tissue</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Sympathetic nerves constricting the local arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blood clotting under the skin</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The wheal is local oedema from increased capillary permeability.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Does the axon reflex need the spinal cord?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Yes: the signal must reach the spinal cord to be relayed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Yes: the brainstem must coordinate the flare</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. No: the signal stays within the branches of one sensory nerve fibre</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. No: the response is caused by histamine alone, with no nerves</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The signal travels from one branch of a sensory neuron back down its other branches, so no central connection is involved.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> A local anaesthetic is injected into the forearm skin before a firm stroke. Which part of the triple response is lost?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The red line along the stroke</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The flare spreading around the line</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The raised wheal along the line</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. All three parts of the response</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The flare depends on the axon reflex, so blocking the nerve stops it. The red line and the wheal come from local injury and histamine.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-chapter-essentials\">4. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Skin blood flow regulates heat</strong>, not just nutrition. It can far exceed what the skin needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Hot → dilate</strong> (lose heat); <strong>cold → sympathetic constriction</strong> (keep heat).</li>\n<li><strong>Arteriovenous anastomoses</strong> in the fingers, toes, ears and nose shunt warm blood near the surface to lose heat.</li>\n<li><strong>Haemorrhage → cold, pale skin</strong> (sympathetic shutdown); early septic shock may give warm skin.</li>\n<li><strong>Triple response:</strong> <strong>red line</strong> (local dilation) → <strong>flare</strong> (axon reflex) → <strong>wheal</strong> (histamine, leaky capillaries, oedema).</li>\n<li><strong>Flare = axon reflex:</strong> a stroked branch sends impulses back down the fibre's other branches, which release substance P and CGRP to dilate arterioles. No spinal cord needed.</li>\n<li>A light stroke gives only a brief <strong>white line</strong> (local constriction), which is not part of the triple response.</li>\n</ol>\n";
